design:
ROOT CAUSE FOUND in src/cli/secret.ts handleSet():

Line ~328-330:
  const msg = JSON.stringify({ type: 'auth', member_name: name, password: secretValue, persist: finalPersist }) + '\n';
  secretValue = '';   // wipes local copy immediately after building the socket message (intentional memory hygiene)
  client.write(msg);

Later, if the socket delivery succeeds (delivered=true, i.e. a live pendingRequests entry existed on the fleet server's auth-socket for this exact credential name -- see src/services/auth-socket.ts submitPassword(), which returns ok:true only when pendingRequests.get(memberName) exists) AND --persist was passed:

  } else if (persist) {
    // OOB delivery + persist: store the secret
    credentialSet(name, secretValue, true, 'allow');   // secretValue is '' here -- already wiped above!

Result: an EMPTY STRING gets persisted to the credential store whenever both (a) a live pending auth request exists for that name (typically because the fleet MCP credential_store_set tool spawned this exact 'secret --set <name>' CLI as its OOB terminal collector -- see auth-socket.ts getAuthCommand()) and (b) --persist is set on the CLI invocation. This matches every observed symptom in this investigation: {{secure.NAME}} resolving to a stray 1-character value or empty string for e2e_gh_token/TEST_E2E_GH/scratch_test, reproduced live with a known throwaway value (set to literal 'ASDF' via credential_store_set OOB flow -- correctly resolved to 4 chars; but 'apra-fleet secret --set scratch_test --persist' run manually against a live listener produced LEN=0).

FIX: capture secretValue in a separate const BEFORE it is wiped for the socket send, and use that captured copy (not the mutated secretValue) in both credentialSet() calls at the bottom of handleSet(). Clear the captured copy after use for the same memory-hygiene reason the original wipe existed.

Also add a regression test: connect to a live pending auth-socket request for a name, invoke handleSet with --persist, and assert the persisted value equals what was sent over the socket, not empty string.

description:
Discovered while fixing apra-fleet-7ll (execute_command structuredContent). Multiple apra-fleet-se test mock fleet APIs (advanced-mock-runner-test.mjs, budget-live.test.mjs, golden-transcript.test.mjs, golden-transcript-3bead.test.mjs) implement their bd/git command mocking as:

  const { err, stdout, stderr } = await runCmd(opts.command, tempDir);
  if (err) return { isError: true, content: [{ text: stderr || err.message }] };
  return { content: [{ text: stdout }] };

runCmd() wraps Node's child_process.exec(), whose callback receives a non-null `err` for ANY nonzero exit code, not just spawn-level failures. So these mocks treat "bd exited nonzero" as an MCP-level isError:true failure.

Real src/tools/execute-command.ts does NOT do this: its synchronous success path (result.code !== 0 branch) still returns a normal (non-isError) response with `Exit code: N\n<output>` text and (after apra-fleet-7ll) structuredContent -- a nonzero shell exit is just data on a successful dispatch, not a dispatch-level error. isError is reserved for actual dispatch failures (credential/network blocks, launch exceptions).

This means these mocks are more lenient in one place (never distinguish spawn failure from nonzero exit) and stricter in another (treat nonzero exit as a hard MCP error) than reality -- any runner.js code path that relies on receiving a normal (non-error) response for a nonzero-exit bd command would pass against these mocks today but could behave differently against the real server. Not yet confirmed to cause an actual runner.js bug; filed for investigation, not proven broken.

Deferred (P3): the immediate apra-fleet-7ll fix does not depend on resolving this; low priority since no concrete runner.js bug has been traced to it yet.
